KD: \par
\par
What level of specificity should we aim for when illustrating the importance of context? On an essay in Paper One, for example, on women, or the significance of gender roles, is it satisfactory just to say '\'85Women were seen as inferior due to the misogyny at the time\'85' or do we need to give illustrative examples? The same goes for Paper Two: can we just talk about the Great Depression and the Dust Bowl in broad terms or will an examiner reward higher marks to a candidate who talks in great levels of specificity when it comes to their context? \par
\par
 \par
\par
AM: \par
\par
The distinction that is to be made here is between contextual reading of the text that remain relevant to the question [Good!] and mini-history essays that lead the candidate away from a discussion of the text [emphatically Bad!]. As ever, this is only going to be meaningful in this subject when exemplified in a specific essay\'85 \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85\lquote One of literature\rquote s most frequent moral lessons is that pride comes before a fall\rquote .  \par
\par
In the light of this view, consider ways in which the texts that you have studied explore the dangers of complacency or arrogance. \par
\par
In your answer, compare one drama and one poetry text from the above lists. [30] \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85It is difficult for us to imagine a culture that regarded ambition \f1\endash  and its related quality pride \endash  as a vice, but a brief glance at the plot of Shakespeare\rquote s Macbeth reveals that the early modern period regarded pride with suspicion. Milton\rquote s 17th century readership would regard Satan as a character whose besetting sin is pride. He casts himself as an innocent victim, overlooked by God, but his inability to think unselfishly, his inability to literally see beyond himself, is his downfall. His confidence in thinking that he could ever overthrow God displays tremendous vanity and pride. It is important to realise that Satan in Paradise Lost is essentially Milton\rquote s own creation \endash  the Bible itself has very little to say about Satan and it is fair to say that he is thinly characterized. Webster\rquote s Cardinal is clearly founded on a familiar trope in early modern English Literature \endash  the Roman Catholic villain. When we see The Cardinal transform himself in Act 3, Scene 4 from a clergyman to a soldier, we need to bear in mind that this was an English Protestant\rquote s worst nightmare in the 17th century.  \par
\par
Both villains are prompted by pride to be disdainful of others\rquote  interests\'85  \par
\par
