KD:  \par
\par
Another question\'85this time one that is a bit more specific for Paper 1: when discussing structural points in the tempest extract in that first 15 marker, can we discuss what happens before and after the scene we have been given in the question? For example, could a structural point in Act 2, Scene 2 be the contrast between the absurd plotting against Prospero\rquote s rule of the island involving Trinculo, Stephano and Caliban, and the malicious and \ldblquote serious\rdblquote  plotting against King Alonso and his regime that develops between Antonio and Sebastian in the previous scene? Or should we focus on the stuff directly in the extract given to us in the paper? \par
\par
 \par
\par
AM: \par
\par
Not only can you \ldblquote discuss what happens before and after the scene we have been given\rdblquote , but this is how I recommend you begin every (a)-type question. Indeed, nearly all of the exemplar answers that I have written for you this year begin this way\'85 \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85They now are in my pow\rquote r\'85 \par
\par
(The Tempest, III.iii.48) \par
\par
 \par
\par
This scene constitutes a kind of dramatic climax in the play\rquote s action: finally, Prospero\rquote s ambitions seem to be realised and he appears to be in complete control of the world around him. Raising the tempest at the start of the play seems to have worked out beautifully and, as he says later, \ldblquote At this hour/Lie at my mercy all mine enemies\'85\rdblquote . It is with a shock, then, that we realise that we are only in Act 3: there are just over two Acts of the play still to go and it is this lack of dramatic jeopardy that gives this play its distinctive tone.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
KD: \par
\par
How do we tell in the passage that we are given in the exam for an (a)-type question if something is in iambic pentameter (blank verse) or in prose? Personally,  I\rquote m not sure I understand the difference between the forms let alone how to identify them. \par
\par
AM: \par
\par
This one is easy: if there are capital letters at the start of the line \endash  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Caliban.\f0\u8239?Be not afeard; the isle is full of noises, \par
Sounds and sweet airs, that give delight and hurt not. \par
Sometimes a thousand twangling instruments \par
Will hum about mine ears, and sometime voices \par
That, if I then had waked after long sleep, \par
Will make me sleep again: and then, in dreaming, \par
The clouds methought would open and show riches \par
Ready to drop upon me that, when I waked, \par
I cried to dream again\'85 \par
\par
(The Tempest, III.ii.135-143) \par
\par
 \par
\par
- then you are dealing with poetry, normally blank verse/iambic pentameter (i.e. a ten-syllable line with stresses falling on the second, fourth, sixth, eight and tenth syllables \f1\endash  \ldblquote ti-tum-ti-tum-ti-tum-ti-tum-ti-tum\rdblquote  goes the beat), while if the speech is laid out on the page without capital letters at the beginning of each line, with capitals being used simply after a full stop \endash  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Trinculo.\f0\u8239?Here's neither bush nor shrub, to bear off any weather at all, and another storm brewing; I hear it sing i' the wind: yond same black cloud, yond huge one, looks like a foul bombard that would shed his liquor. If it should thunder as it did before, I know not where to hide my head: yond same cloud cannot choose but fall by pailfuls. What have we here? a man or a fish? dead or alive? A fish: he smells like a fish; a very ancient and fish - like smell; a kind of not of the newest Poor-John. A strange fish! Were I in England now, as once I was, and had but this fish painted, not a holiday fool there but would give a piece \par
of silver: there would this monster make a man; any strange beast there makes a man: when they will not give a doit to relieve a lame beggar, they will lazy out ten to see a dead Indian... \par
\par
(The Tempest, II.ii.18-28)  \par
\par
 \par
\par
- then you are dealing with prose. In Shakespeare, the distinction between poetry and prose is important as it marks a social, and therefore, a moral distinction. Caliban is capable of blank verse; Trinculo and Stephano are not.    \par
\par
Very occasionally, other verse forms appear in The Tempest \f1\endash  Stephano sings a rude sea shanty, the masque scene (IV.i) features a tetrameter sequence sung by Juno, but, as in all Shakespeare plays, the verse moves to an iambic (heart)beat. \f0\u8239? \par
\par
