TB:   \par
Just wondering, could you remind me of the moments in Paradise Lost, Book X please? I want to make sure I\rquote ve got enough for it. \par
\par
AM: \par
\par
We looked carefully at the scene in Pandaemonium when Satan returns to Hell, expecting to be lauded for the destruction of humanity and he is, instead, hissed by the devils: Paradise Lost, X.414-465. You can find it here, in the best (i.e. most easily accessible) online version of the text\'85Paradise Lost: Book 10. We also looked at the end of Book X  \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85they [i.e. Adam and Eve] forthwith to the place \par
Repairing where he\u8239?judged\u8239?them prostrate fell \par
Before him reverent, and both\u8239?confessed\u8239? \par
Humbly\u8239?their\u8239?faults, and pardon\u8239?begged, with tears \par
Watering the ground, and with\u8239?their\u8239?sighs the air \par
Frequenting, sent from hearts contrite, in sign \par
Of sorrow\u8239?unfeigned, and humiliation meek. \par
\par
(Paradise Lost, X.1098-1104) \par
\par
 \par
\par
as it provides us with a wonderful contrast with end of Book IX \f1\endash  \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85Thus they in mutual accusation spent \par
The fruitless hours, but neither self-condemning, \par
And of\f0\u8239?their\u8239?vain contest\u8239?appeared\u8239?no end. \par
\par
(Paradise Lost, IX.1187-1189) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Apart from these passages, it might be worth looking at the Argument so you know what\rquote s going on, but no examiner is going to look for references from both Books: that simply isn\rquote t one of the criteria on the basis of which your answer will be judged. \par
\par
